WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.970
Vsauce! Kevin here -- and I want to go over
a real-life math mistake that ended up actually

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.590
making a murderer.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:14.379
Our top story tonight: she seemed to be the
woman with everything - a good job, a lovely

00:00:14.379 --> 00:00:17.970
house, and the beginnings of a young family.
But tonight, the life of Cheshire solicitor

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.890
Sally Clark is in tatters after she was convicted
of killing her two baby sons. Two children,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:35.679
both dead before their first birthday. Sally
Clark, the mother, is arrested. Charged with

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:37.980
murder.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:44.350
What happens when two improbable tragedies
happen to the same person? How do you calculate

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:51.730
odds to differentiate unbelievably bad luck
from murder? And what happens when you make

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:57.969
a conditional probability error so severe
that it decimates countless lives?

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:04.720
I’ve talked about a lot of paradoxes and
math problems that, honestly, are kind of…

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:11.280
theoretical. They’re REAL, but the situations
involving them don’t come up a whole lot.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.190
You’re not THAT likely to be a game show
contestant or have to figure out whether you

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.610
should go first in Russian Roulette.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.490
And it doesn’t really matter whether you
can play a dot game with perfect efficiency.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:30.610
It’s awesome to know how it works and why,
but it’s ultimately not life or death.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:35.810
But... some math is life or death.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:42.980
On February 23, 1998, Sally and Steve Clark
were arrested and charged with murder a month

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:49.280
after their infant son was found dead. He
was only 2 months old. Sally and Steve claimed

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.700
that he had died naturally, a case of Sudden
Infant Death Syndrome -- also called ‘crib

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.040
death’ or ‘cot death’ -- that couldn’t
possibly have been prevented. The baby had

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.780
physical trauma including a cracked rib, which
his parents claimed were from resuscitation

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:06.780
efforts.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.180
So… You can see why the police would investigate.
The real problem -- the thing that makes the

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.660
case notable, and where the math comes in
-- is that it wasn’t the first time one

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.209
of the Clarks’ infants had been found dead.

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:28.880
Almost the exact same thing happened with
their first child about a year earlier.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:36.030
So what are the odds that an incredibly rare
tragedy would happen twice in the same family,

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:42.760
like… the horrible opposite of winning the
lottery more than once? And the major question

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:51.650
is, what are the odds of that compared to
the odds that one or both of those children

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:52.709
were murdered?

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:58.909
Well, there was an expert for exactly this
sort of thing. Sir Roy Meadow was a pediatrician

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:06.040
who built a fantastic reputation on his 1977
paper outlining Munchausen Syndrome by Proxy

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:12.999
-- where a caregiver secretly makes someone
sick so they’re permanently dependent, or

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:18.969
sometimes just to be seen as heroic and selfless
by making the sacrifice to care for them.

00:03:18.969 --> 00:03:26.299
By the early 1990’s, Meadow had become a
recognized authority on child abuse. He literally

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:33.129
wrote the book on it -- “The ABCs of Child
Abuse” -- and testified at dozens of trials

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:35.650
involving allegedly abusive parents.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:41.760
It’s how we got “Meadow’s Law,” the
maxim Sir Roy used to summarize his attitudes

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:48.999
toward abuse cases: “One is a tragedy, two
is suspicious and three is murder unless there

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:51.329
is proof to the contrary."

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:56.799
And that’s where we find our first red flag
-- he lifted his “law” from the James

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:59.139
Bond villain Goldfinger.

00:03:59.139 --> 00:04:04.430
By now, we’ve discarded Meadow’s Law,
and Sir Roy Meadow’s legacy is completely

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:10.859
tarnished -- because he either didn’t know
conditional probability or he didn’t care,

00:04:10.859 --> 00:04:13.629
and Sally Clark paid the price.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:19.209
Here’s how conditional probability actually
works. It’s figuring out the chances of

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:24.580
something happening given that something else
has already occurred. It’s the probability

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:31.669
of A, then B. Sometimes that’s a succession
of totally unrelated events, like coin flips.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:36.970
Whether I get a YES or a NO on the second
coin flip doesn’t depend on what I got the

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:43.871
first time. It’s the odds of THIS times
the odds of THAT, and we get a value for the

00:04:43.871 --> 00:04:49.910
chances of both independent things happening
together despite neither one having ANYTHING

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.650
to do with the other.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:57.870
Let’s take cards. The odds of drawing a
heart are 1 in 4. Odds of drawing a second

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:04.449
heart? 12 in 51… cuz we just took one out
of the deck. Two hearts in a row is just ¼

00:05:04.449 --> 00:05:13.200
* 12/51 = 12 in 204. That’s easy -- and
thinking that independent events somehow influence

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.040
each other when they don’t? That is the
famous gambler’s fallacy. You don’t wanna

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.620
fall for that.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:26.200
But sometimes two events are related in some
way, and figuring out the odds of both of

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.440
them happening together gets trickier.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:36.419
Like, the average chance of getting into a
car accident is around 1 in 350 for every

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:43.080
1,000 miles you drive. So, what are the odds
of getting into two accidents in two months,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:49.360
assuming you drive 1,000 miles a month? Do
you just do some multiplication for two independent

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.780
events, 1/350 * 1/350 to get 1 in 122,500?

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:03.419
Ehhhh… You COULD do that, and that’s an
okay place to start -- but what if you’re

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:08.780
the worst driver in the world? What if you’ve
only been driving for a couple of weeks? What

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:12.849
if it’s winter and everything is icy? Is
there a difference between driving in Los

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:19.010
Angeles and… Wyoming? Or what if you’re
a Fast &amp; Furious driver and just had the unluckiest

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:25.790
couple of months of your life? As the information
changes, the math changes.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:32.669
And this is all common sense -- so how did
Roy Meadow completely mess this up?

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:37.560
During the trial, Meadow cited a report that
established the chances of an infant dying

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.700
from Sudden Infant Death Syndrome using all
sorts of different risk factors, like the

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:48.860
mother being young or being a smoker. Sally
Clark was a healthy woman with a good standard

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:54.599
of living -- she was a lawyer -- so the chances
of someone in her circumstances suffering

00:06:54.599 --> 00:07:03.310
a SIDS tragedy was calculated at 1 in 8,543
-- and this is where Meadow’s math went

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.870
horribly wrong.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:14.879
He just multiplied 1/8,543 by 1/8,543 to get
the chances of the two independent events

00:07:14.879 --> 00:07:24.000
occurring twice for the same mother -- which
comes out to 1 in 73 million. Given the United

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.009
Kingdom’s birthrate, Meadow said it’s
the sort of coincidence that would happen

00:07:28.009 --> 00:07:35.849
once every century. He literally treated Sally
Clark’s deceased children like playing cards

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:41.659
or coin flips instead of human beings living
in complex circumstances.

00:07:41.659 --> 00:07:50.610
Here’s a question: What does 1 in 73 million
LOOK like? In 2014, Tim Urban created an image

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:59.719
of one million dots. So here’s 73 of those.
And as of 2021, only 19 of the 235 countries

00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:04.379
in the world have a population over 73 million.

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:09.999
You could make an entire documentary series
out of all the medical evidence to consider

00:08:09.999 --> 00:08:16.999
in this trial -- but Meadow’s testimony
reduced all of the complexities into something

00:08:16.999 --> 00:08:24.310
the court could easily understand: Two accidents
like this are so statistically improbable

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:30.979
that it makes way more sense to think she
killed her kids.

00:08:30.979 --> 00:08:36.360
That type of faulty logic actually has a name
-- the Prosecutor’s Fallacy -- which is

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:43.860
misusing statistics to create the impression
that the accused has to have done the crime.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:48.550
Sometimes that fallacy’s used by accident,
sometimes it’s used on purpose.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:54.200
But in this case, you can’t even give Meadow
the benefit of the doubt and say he misspoke

00:08:54.200 --> 00:09:00.240
or just wasn’t thinking -- because he even
compared the odds of both Clark children dying

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:08.829
naturally to someone betting successfully
on 80-to-1 longshot horses for 4 races in

00:09:08.829 --> 00:09:09.839
a row.

00:09:09.839 --> 00:09:16.100
The problem is saying something only has a
1 in 73 million chance of occurring naturally

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:23.639
suggests that it’s way more likely the explanation
is simpler. For Sally Clark, that meant being

00:09:23.639 --> 00:09:26.300
guilty of infanticide.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:32.260
What’s truly weird to me about this whole
story is, people don’t ALWAYS assume the

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:37.620
worst just because the odds of something happening
are crazy. When your Grandma Ethel hits a

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.820
1 in a million jackpot on a casino slot machine,
you think, “Oh my god, yay, she is INSANELY

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:50.709
lucky!” We DON'T immediately suspect her
of being a cheater. We think it's amazing,

00:09:50.709 --> 00:09:57.680
not utterly impossible. But we think about
crimes differently -- someone’s been charged

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.529
and the suspicion is baked in.

00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:09.300
If you’re a juror, isn’t 1 in 73 million
WAY way beyond a reasonable doubt?

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:14.350
Beyond defective math, there was a lot going
on here -- like the Clarks’ baby wearing

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:19.310
an apnea monitor that would go off if the
baby stopped breathing. That was standard

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:25.220
practice after a family suffered a SIDS death
in the past. The child’s monitor kept going

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:30.490
off in the months prior to his death, and
public health officials said the unit was

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:36.839
faulty and had it replaced. Did that unit
actually malfunction? Is there a chance they

00:10:36.839 --> 00:10:39.600
misread warning signs?

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.960
As we add layer upon layer of complexity,
simple statistics are even more appealing

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:54.149
as a clean explanation for a difficult problem.
It’s not hard to see that an entire trial’s

00:10:54.149 --> 00:11:00.970
worth of facts could boil down to one question:
Is it the 1 in 73 million chance that Sally

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:10.829
Clark was the unluckiest mother in Britain,
or one of the other 72,999,999 outcomes? And

00:11:10.829 --> 00:11:17.139
whether or not you’re misled by the implications
of that statistic makes all the difference.

00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:24.730
Sally Clark was convicted of murder by a vote
of 10-2, and she was given a mandatory life

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:29.449
sentence. Newspapers reported that she was
guilty of killing her babies. They called

00:11:29.449 --> 00:11:36.660
her Baby Killer, Baby Murderer, and Killer
Mum. They described her as a “Tortured Soul.”

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:42.410
Her husband Steve insisted that she was innocent.
Her appeal was dismissed because the court

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:47.850
claimed the statistical errors were quote,
“[in]capable of affecting the safety of

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:54.000
the convictions.” Roy Meadow backed them
up, saying the time spent on an appeal was

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:00.339
quote, “wasted.” But the Royal Statistical
Society blasted Meadow and the court for butchering

00:12:00.339 --> 00:12:02.970
such basic math stating:

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:09.060
“...there is a real possibility that without
proper guidance, and well-informed presentation,

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:14.220
frequency estimates presented in court could
be misinterpreted by the jury in ways that

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.579
are very prejudicial to defendants.

00:12:17.579 --> 00:12:23.320
Society does not tolerate doctors making serious
clinical errors because it is widely understood

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:26.959
that
such errors could mean the difference between

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:31.980
life and death. The case of R v. Sally Clark
is one

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:38.070
example of a medical expert witness making
a serious statistical error, one which may

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:43.019
have had a
profound effect on the outcome of the case.”

00:12:43.019 --> 00:12:49.110
Sally Clark appealed again. Not only was the
statistical evidence flawed, but her lawyers

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:55.690
discovered medical documents showing her son
actually died from a serious bacterial infection.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:13:03.560
The math was wrong and there was now a rock-solid
medical explanation for his natural death.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:09.660
5 years after the trial, analysis in the journal
Paediatric and Perinatal Epidemiology showed

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:17.449
that as rare as double SIDS is, double infanticide
is even more rare -- making double SIDS anywhere

00:13:17.449 --> 00:13:24.250
from 4.5 to 9 times more likely than double
infant murder.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:30.840
Sally Clark was released in 2003 -- and between
the grief of losing two children in tragic

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:37.300
circumstances, being wrongly imprisoned for
3 years, and having the entire United Kingdom

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:44.139
think she was the worst mother in the world,
she died of alcohol poisoning just 4 years

00:13:44.139 --> 00:13:49.070
later. She left behind her husband and their
third child.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:54.500
The fallout was immense. The British General
Medical Council removed Roy Meadow from the

00:13:54.500 --> 00:14:00.490
British Medical Register. Cases in which Meadow
testified were revisited, and the High Court

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:06.459
ruled that expert witnesses were not immune
to disciplinary action. He was barred from

00:14:06.459 --> 00:14:11.399
being an expert witness, endured a series
of hearings about professional misconduct,

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:16.730
and by 2009 he was not licensed to practice
medicine.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:24.199
Roy Meadow should have known how basic probability
worked. You know and I know -- so we should

00:14:24.199 --> 00:14:31.590
expect a highly-educated medical professional
willing to testify in court to know, too.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:37.830
But he also blew it on the science -- his
misleading statistics dismissed the possibility

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:45.279
of any cause of death that couldn’t currently
be identified, like an unknown genetic defect.

00:14:45.279 --> 00:14:54.260
Instead, he multiplied two fractions because
it was easy. And most of the courtroom bought

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:55.260
it.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:15:01.130
Whether people think math is boring or it’s
a waste of time to think through theoretical

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:09.379
concepts, the fact is: we learn the theoretical
so that we know how to solve problems in reality

00:15:09.379 --> 00:15:13.860
-- and sometimes to avoid terrible tragedies.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:17.390
It’s why we have to understand.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:24.420
Look. There’s an old conditional probability
joke about a statistician who said that he

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:30.470
always flies with a bomb in his suitcase.
When someone incredulously asked him why,

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:36.060
he said, “Because the odds of TWO people
having bombs on the same flight are astronomical!”

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:42.900
If you get why that joke is funny, you get
conditional probability -- and if that courtroom

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:47.350
knew how it worked, Sally Clark would probably
be alive today.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:48.410
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:16:00.180
Today is not a victory. We are not victorious.
There are no winners here. We have all lost.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:16.599
We simply feel relief that our nightmare is
finally at an end.

